4. QUIETWALL

FUNCTION

An architectural wall panel specifically engineered to manage sound.

Not simply:

"Put fiberglass behind drywall."

The wall itself becomes an acoustic assembly.

---

4.1 Acoustic Strategy

Three different acoustic problems must be separated:

ABSORPTION

Reduce reflected sound inside a room.

MASS

Reduce airborne sound transmission through the wall.

DECOUPLING

Prevent structural vibration from crossing the assembly.

A serious Quietwall therefore requires multiple layers.

---

4.2 Layer Stack

FINISH SKIN

↓

ACOUSTIC ABSORBER

↓

STRUCTURAL AIRSPACE

↓

MASS BARRIER

↓

DECOUPLING LAYER

↓

STRUCTURAL FRAME

The exact sequence depends on whether the objective is room acoustics or transmission loss.

---

4.3 Absorber

Candidate materials:

- mineral wool
- recycled textile fiber
- cellulose-based acoustic material
- engineered porous mineral
- bio-based acoustic composites

Selection should consider:

- acoustic coefficient
- moisture resistance
- fire performance
- density
- long-term settling

---

4.4 Mass Layer

Candidate:

- mineral composite panel
- dense polymer/mineral composite
- engineered board
- metal composite

This is where the system can depart substantially from drywall.

A dense, dimensionally stable panel can provide mass without being fragile gypsum board.

---

4.5 Decoupling

Use:

- elastomeric mounts
- resilient channels
- isolated attachment points

Critical rule:

DON'T·BUILD·A·BRIDGE·AROUND·YOUR·DECOUPLER.

One accidental rigid connection can substantially compromise the intended acoustic isolation.

---

4.6 Acoustic Surface

Replaceable surface cartridges could include:

- perforated panel
- microperforated panel
- fabric acoustic surface
- slatted diffuser
- hard reflective architectural panel

Thus one wall can be tuned for:

ABSORPTION

or

DIFFUSION

or

ISOLATION

depending upon the room.

